      "content": "While macroeconomic growth is a valid perspective, framing it as an overall \"success\" overlooks the human cost borne by specific working-class segments. Economic growth generated by globalization often trickles down unevenly, failing to compensate for lost manufacturing jobs. Thus, you can argue that while macro-level economic statistics might look positive, the agreement failed in its implicit social contract to protect labor stability. This allows you to address both the \"big picture\" while maintaining your argument on domestic impact.",
