      "content": "A nuanced conclusion can acknowledge consumer benefits while critiquing labor outcomes; this avoids a monolithic \"failure\" label. You can argue success exists only in niche sectors, such as those benefiting from increased exports or higher-skilled service jobs created by the trade. However, this perspective must be framed cautiously, arguing that these localized gains were insufficient to offset the widespread deindustrialization experienced by the broader, traditional blue-collar base. Therefore, it was a partial success at best, failing the working class as a whole.",
      "source": "target",
